1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:08,223
Watch the brush. A small square of stone appears, with a bull and a row of signs that nobody can read.

2
00:00:08,223 --> 00:00:14,610
This is a seal from Mohenjo-daro, a city on the Indus that flourished about four thousand five hundred years ago.

3
00:00:14,610 --> 00:00:17,972
Who lived here, and what was their city like?

4
00:00:28,783 --> 00:00:36,996
How do we know? For this age, almost everything comes from the ground. Archaeologists dug up the cities themselves.

5
00:00:36,996 --> 00:00:41,011
The seals do carry writing, but no one has been able to read it.

6
00:00:41,011 --> 00:00:45,953
There are no travellers' accounts, no coins, and no books from this time.

7
00:00:55,250 --> 00:01:05,559
Excavation began in 1921, when Daya Ram Sahni dug at Harappa, on the river Ravi. In 1922, R. D.

8
00:01:05,559 --> 00:01:09,217
Banerji began at Mohenjo-daro, on the Indus.

9
00:01:09,217 --> 00:01:16,731
In 1924, John Marshall, who headed the Archaeological Survey, announced the discovery to the world.

10
00:01:16,731 --> 00:01:24,112
Since then, archaeologists have found hundreds of sites. Harappa and Mohenjo-daro are in Pakistan.

11
00:01:24,112 --> 00:01:32,903
Kalibangan is in Rajasthan, Rakhigarhi is in Haryana, and Lothal and Dholavira are in Gujarat. Look at the plan of a city.

12
00:01:32,903 --> 00:01:36,891
The streets run straight, and cross at right angles, in a grid.

13
00:01:36,891 --> 00:01:42,677
The houses were built of baked bricks of a standard size, in the ratio four to two to one.

14
00:01:42,677 --> 00:01:48,441
Harappa and Mohenjo-daro each had a raised citadel to the west, and a lower town to the east.

15
00:01:48,441 --> 00:01:57,555
On the citadel at Mohenjo-daro stands the Great Bath, a large brick tank. Dholavira is different. It has three parts.

16
00:01:57,555 --> 00:02:05,091
Now look below the street. Many houses had a bathing room, and wastewater ran into covered drains along the streets.

17
00:02:05,091 --> 00:02:09,058
Careful drainage like this is one of the best-known marks of these cities.

18
00:02:09,058 --> 00:02:17,797
At Lothal, in Gujarat, there is a large brick basin, often called a dockyard. It suggests that these cities traded by sea.

19
00:02:17,797 --> 00:02:21,017
Trade is the story of the next episode.

20
00:02:30,317 --> 00:02:38,821
Old name, new name. The first cities were found along the Indus, so for years this was called the Indus Valley Civilisation.

21
00:02:38,821 --> 00:02:42,397
Today the name Harappan Civilisation is widely used.

22
00:02:42,397 --> 00:02:49,202
Archaeologists often name a culture after the first site found, and many sites lie far from the Indus.

23
00:02:58,517 --> 00:03:05,572
Carve this tip into memory. Grid streets, covered drains, Great Bath, unread seals.

24
00:03:05,572 --> 00:03:11,085
If you can recall those four, you can describe a Harappan city in an answer.

25
00:03:20,383 --> 00:03:34,984
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Look at this small square of stone, carved with a bull and signs. What is it called?

26
00:03:34,984 --> 00:03:48,425
The answer: a seal. Number two. Look at this raised part of the city, where the Great Bath stood. What is it called?

27
00:03:48,425 --> 00:04:00,449
The answer: the citadel. Number three. Look at this tool for weighing goods. What is it called?

28
00:04:00,449 --> 00:04:10,119
The answer: a balance with weights. Now one line for Mains. Describe the town planning of the Harappan cities.

29
00:04:23,417 --> 00:04:31,194
The seal goes into its case. It is made of steatite, a soft stone, and it is about four thousand five hundred years old.

30
00:04:31,194 --> 00:04:39,172
Three facts to take away. The cities had grid streets and covered drains. The script on the seals is still unread.

31
00:04:39,172 --> 00:04:48,477
And the mature phase lasted from about 2600 to 1900 BCE. Next gallery: Life in an Indus City.

